    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-212054",
      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3a≡2\\cdot 3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=2$ and the modulus $n=16$ are not coprime since $\\text{gcd}(2,16)=2.$ As a result, we cannot cancel out $2$ from both sides of the congruence.\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $2$ and $16$ are $\\text{not coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is not equivalent to}$ $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$",
